WEBVTT

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.750
So now that you have set up and fully

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:04.070
understand the ARMS framework and you

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:06.070
have your skills, you have your memory,

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:08.230
your routines and applications

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:10.629
integrated into your own workspace, what

00:00:10.639 --> 00:00:13.270
you can now do as an option is set up a

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.430
second brain system. So in the following

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:17.590
lesson, what I'll be going through is my

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:20.070
own second brain system, which I use

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:22.630
called Fable 5 to build. But if in case

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:24.630
you don't have access to Fable 5 or if

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:26.310
it's too expensive for you, what I've

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:28.310
done is import this whole system as well

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.230
as this visual so that you can just send

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:31.990
it to your agent and it will build it

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.830
out for you without having to spend a

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:35.510
lot of the token cost. Also, when you

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:36.950
watch this lesson, I want you to pay

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.630
attention more to the principles of

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.310
building the second brain system because

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.150
the more and more that you use these

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:44.150
agentic platforms, the more that you'll

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.069
also find out that each of our

00:00:46.079 --> 00:00:47.910
workspaces and the way we work with

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:49.670
these agents are actually quite

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:50.950
different. So that's something that I

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:53.110
always tell our community because the

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:55.029
way that I build my own workspace or the

00:00:55.039 --> 00:00:57.110
way that I use cloud for my workspace

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:58.790
may be different from yours. So even if

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.389
I'm providing you the templates, what I

00:01:00.399 --> 00:01:02.310
want you to take away as nuggets in this

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:04.710
lesson is how I build it out. So that

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:06.469
the next time that you are actually

00:01:06.479 --> 00:01:08.070
tasked with building something out like

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:09.750
this, you can apply the same principles

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:11.429
and techniques that I will talk about so

00:01:11.439 --> 00:01:13.190
that you're a bit more flexible when it

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.670
comes to your builds. Enjoy. So what

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:17.030
we're looking at right now is the

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:18.870
entirety of the second brain that Claude

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:21.429
Fable 5 made for me. So I have files for

00:01:21.439 --> 00:01:23.350
my business work. I have files for

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:25.270
content because I do a lot of content

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:27.830
stuff for personal as well as for my

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:29.190
community. And you can see in this

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:31.109
second brain system you have these

00:01:31.119 --> 00:01:32.630
departments similar to what you would

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:34.310
expect let's say if you are building out

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:36.310
a company second brain. But when I was

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:37.830
building out this system along with

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:39.670
Fable, I actually wanted to make sure

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:42.149
that this system and this visual is

00:01:42.159 --> 00:01:44.310
useful as well because let's say you're

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:46.710
using a default tool for graphing your

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:48.469
nodes, your second brain, similar to

00:01:48.479 --> 00:01:49.670
what you would expect from something

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:51.590
like Obsidian, which is probably the

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:53.190
most popular one in the market right

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:55.350
now. To me, a data visualization chart

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:57.350
like this isn't really useful if it

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:58.950
doesn't tell you any information about

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:00.310
what you're seeing here. More often than

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:01.910
not, what I'm finding is for people

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:03.910
using obsidian as their second brain and

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:06.310
this graph view, it is merely just for

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:07.830
show and just for illustrating that you

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:09.350
have this fancy connections in your

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:11.190
nodes. But for this version of the

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:13.270
second brain, apart from it just being a

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:15.510
bit more visual and giving me clear view

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:17.670
of let's say how many more files I have

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:20.550
in my business department versus that of

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:22.630
content versus that of my personal

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:24.309
workspace. It also gives me a clearer

00:02:24.319 --> 00:02:26.550
view of my entire agentic operating

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:28.470
system which at least for me the way I

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:30.710
teach it is if you get these four

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:32.949
aspects right your applications your

00:02:32.959 --> 00:02:35.270
routines your memory as well as your

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.430
skills then you are definitely going to

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:41.190
be ahead of 99% of the users of agentic

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:42.869
tools like cloud code and so here on the

00:02:42.879 --> 00:02:44.630
blue line you can see which particular

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:46.550
applications I have connected to my

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:48.390
cloud code whether that's via an MCP

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:51.190
connector an API or a CLI tool and you

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:53.030
can see when Fable 5 made this for me It

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:54.550
identified that I have it connected to

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:56.309
Google calendar. I have it connected to

00:02:56.319 --> 00:02:58.390
Google Drive and so on and so forth. And

00:02:58.400 --> 00:02:59.830
the reason why that's important is

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:01.830
because this application view, it gives

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:03.990
you visibility on two things. The first

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:05.830
is if you have an application that you

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:08.390
are using constantly that is not yet in

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.710
this blue circle, then that might mean

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:12.710
that you would need to do some work in

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:14.630
order to plug that in to cloud code in

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:16.550
order to automate that tool. And so in

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:17.990
principle, the more nodes that you have

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.309
in this application layer, the more

00:03:20.319 --> 00:03:22.309
powerful cloud code becomes. But that

00:03:22.319 --> 00:03:23.910
also has a flip side to it, right?

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:25.350
Because the number of nodes that you

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:26.949
have in your application layer here

00:03:26.959 --> 00:03:29.190
doesn't only signify how powerful cloud

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:31.110
code is, it also shows the level of

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:33.670
trust and correspondingly the risk that

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:35.589
you would have when using these agentic

00:03:35.599 --> 00:03:37.270
platforms. Because with a lot of these

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:38.869
tools, let's say you're connected to

00:03:38.879 --> 00:03:40.630
HubSpot for instance, what that

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:42.550
basically means is that if Cloud Code

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:45.589
can connect and control 100% your

00:03:45.599 --> 00:03:48.229
HubSpot workspace, then it'll be able to

00:03:48.239 --> 00:03:50.309
send emails and marketing campaigns to

00:03:50.319 --> 00:03:52.149
your mailing list, which can be risky by

00:03:52.159 --> 00:03:53.589
itself if you don't know what you're

00:03:53.599 --> 00:03:55.110
doing. And so the second way by which

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:56.789
this application layer is going to be

00:03:56.799 --> 00:03:58.949
useful is if there's an application that

00:03:58.959 --> 00:04:01.509
you are actually not using anymore, then

00:04:01.519 --> 00:04:03.509
it might be worth disconnecting that to

00:04:03.519 --> 00:04:05.830
cloud code. And this nice graph view

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:07.589
actually gives you that whole picture so

00:04:07.599 --> 00:04:09.190
that you can just quickly eyeball which

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:11.350
particular applications that yourself or

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:12.949
your business or your client would need

00:04:12.959 --> 00:04:14.710
to disconnect to. Now that's the same

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:16.390
principle when it comes to routines.

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:17.909
Because routines, what are those? They

00:04:17.919 --> 00:04:19.990
are basically your scheduled tasks that

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:21.909
are running in the background. And I've

00:04:21.919 --> 00:04:23.350
talked about this in several other

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:25.270
videos before, but the use of this

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:27.590
yellow area is quite similar to what you

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:29.030
would expect with the applications layer

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:30.710
as well. If you have a lot of routine

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:32.230
set up, then that means that you have a

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:34.070
lot of automation set up. So it may mean

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:36.070
also that you are saving a lot of time.

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:37.590
However, if you have a lot of routines

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:39.590
in here and you actually don't remember

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:41.510
anymore you setting this up and you

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:42.870
actually don't need this particular

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:44.710
automation anymore, then it might be

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:46.390
worth retiring it as well. That is at

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:48.469
least for me personally is running on my

00:04:48.479 --> 00:04:50.310
Hermes agent, which if you need a quick

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:52.070
reminder of what that does, you can see

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:54.550
that it is connected to this skill for

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:56.790
the daily log skill that actually I can

00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:59.670
open that file directly from my file

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:02.070
explorer here on Windows. Or if I zoom

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:04.150
in here and actually look at the skill,

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:06.710
then I can actually read the skill and

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:08.710
what it contains straight from this

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:10.230
second brain view. And then of course

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:11.990
you have your memory layer or the

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:13.830
context that you have built up for your

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:15.830
business over time. So for me

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:17.749
personally, I have roughly and I didn't

00:05:17.759 --> 00:05:19.430
know this apparently I already have

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:21.749
35,466

00:05:21.759 --> 00:05:24.310
files in this folder and Fable was able

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:26.230
to map those out and actually create

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:28.230
relationships between those nodes and

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:29.830
those files. So now let's say I'm

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:31.830
looking for a particular file then I can

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:34.310
just fully replace my file explorer or

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:36.070
my finder in order to just find the

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:37.909
specific document that I'm after

00:05:37.919 --> 00:05:40.469
straight from within this second brain

00:05:40.479 --> 00:05:41.909
system. So let's say I'm looking for

00:05:41.919 --> 00:05:43.990
this document or this photo which I

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:45.590
already know the file name of and I

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:47.350
don't really need Claude to find it for

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:49.270
me. Then it can also show me that and I

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:51.110
can even open it straight from here and

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:53.029
it will open that on my device. So you

00:05:53.039 --> 00:05:55.270
can see this is just a 5x5 grid of

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:57.110
profile photos in there that we can use.

00:05:57.120 --> 00:05:58.790
And then obviously and the last layer

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:00.469
which is probably the most important and

00:06:00.479 --> 00:06:02.710
most useful are the skills. So in this

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:05.110
one view you have a clear visibility of

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:07.510
what your skills are and similar to what

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:09.350
I showed before you can also open them

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:12.150
and see which files or routines they are

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:13.590
connected to. And so with that

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:14.950
framework, if you're building this for

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:17.029
someone else or for a client, then you

00:06:17.039 --> 00:06:19.029
can cleanly and clearly illustrate to

00:06:19.039 --> 00:06:21.110
them how their whole agentic operating

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:23.270
system works, how their second brain

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:25.110
system is set up, and what are the

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:27.749
different departments or fields of work

00:06:27.759 --> 00:06:30.070
that their current workspace or second

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:32.550
brain is operating against. And you can

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:34.230
even take them through actual files in

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:36.309
here and click through it in order to

00:06:36.319 --> 00:06:39.270
expand any of the folders that you may

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:40.710
be interested in. And if it's a

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:42.230
sufficiently good visualization like

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:44.230
this, just like what Cloud Fable did for

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:46.070
us, then I think it is also pretty

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:47.909
useful, right? Because this is probably

00:06:47.919 --> 00:06:50.150
a much better way to communicate the

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:51.909
idea of a second brain system and how

00:06:51.919 --> 00:06:53.590
your whole workspace looks like versus

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:55.990
just showing literally a folder in your

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:57.909
workspace, which for me, that whole

00:06:57.919 --> 00:06:59.749
second brain system that just represents

00:06:59.759 --> 00:07:01.430
all of the files and all of the folders

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:03.350
that they have in this robo workspace.

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:04.629
So, if you're taking someone through

00:07:04.639 --> 00:07:06.870
their workspace, it is probably much

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:08.870
better and much easier to take them

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:11.110
through those ideas in this view rather

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:12.950
than that folder structure. But no

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:15.990
matter how good or fancy or flashy this

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:18.309
visualization is, I would say that it

00:07:18.319 --> 00:07:20.790
only captures around 30% of the value of

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:22.710
a second brain system. Because the other

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:25.430
70% has to do with the day-to-day work

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:27.510
that you ask Claude to do for you. And a

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:29.430
question you should ask yourself is with

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:31.110
this second brain system that I'm

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:33.029
implementing, am I actually getting cla

00:07:33.039 --> 00:07:35.749
to retrieve documents from our memory,

00:07:35.759 --> 00:07:38.150
from our second brain much faster? And

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:39.990
often more importantly, is it getting

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:42.230
those at a lower token cost? Because if

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:43.670
you set up this whole second brain

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:45.830
system, but it either slows you down or

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:47.909
it ends up costing you much more just to

00:07:47.919 --> 00:07:49.749
find files in here, then it's probably

00:07:49.759 --> 00:07:51.510
not worth setting up. And at least for

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:53.270
me, with this system that Fable has set

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:55.350
up, it was able to do those two things.

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:57.510
It was able to retrieve things faster as

00:07:57.520 --> 00:07:59.350
well as for cheaper. And I'll show you

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:01.430
how it works and how I guided Fable to

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:02.869
create the system for me in a bit. But

00:08:02.879 --> 00:08:04.710
just to show it with a quick test, what

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:07.029
I have here are two cloud code sessions.

00:08:07.039 --> 00:08:08.710
This one already has that second brain

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:11.110
system implemented and this blue one

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:13.189
doesn't. So what I'll do now is just

00:08:13.199 --> 00:08:14.790
send these two messages, these two

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:16.230
prompts at the same time. And then by

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:18.230
the end of it, we will see which of them

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:20.629
will get the answer fastest and number

00:08:20.639 --> 00:08:22.629
two if we are saving tokens using this

00:08:22.639 --> 00:08:24.230
second brain system. And there you go.

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:26.710
At least for speed, this one already has

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:29.670
the answer for us. While the one that is

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:31.350
using the default cloud code without

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:33.110
that second brain system is still

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:35.029
channeling and thinking through it and

00:08:35.039 --> 00:08:37.430
basically using these default finders

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:40.149
like GP and Glob in order to find your

00:08:40.159 --> 00:08:41.829
files for you. And when both of those

00:08:41.839 --> 00:08:43.430
tasks have finished, what I can do now

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:45.990
is just type in /context in order to

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:47.990
just find out how much tokens each of

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:49.750
these have used. So the way to read this

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:51.670
in case it's your first time seeing it

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:53.910
is that these system prompts, tools,

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:55.829
memory files, and skills, those are

00:08:55.839 --> 00:08:57.910
pre-injected on every session that you

00:08:57.920 --> 00:08:59.670
start. That's why they are mostly fixed

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:01.269
as you can see. But the messages here,

00:09:01.279 --> 00:09:03.430
this purple area, that is what Claude

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:05.350
actually did in that session. And you

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:06.870
can see for me with this second brain

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:08.870
system, it only took around 30,000

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:11.110
tokens to get to the answer. While for

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:13.509
the default Claude code, it took around

00:09:13.519 --> 00:09:16.550
50,000. So there's roughly around a 40%

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:18.310
savings there, which is pretty good. Now

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:20.230
I did also ask Fable to of course do a

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:21.829
few more tests just to verify that and

00:09:21.839 --> 00:09:23.750
it basically spun up that same test for

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:25.509
default cloud code versus the second

00:09:25.519 --> 00:09:26.870
brain system that it built and it's

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:28.710
passing those criteria for each of them.

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:30.230
Now because each of our workspace is

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:31.350
actually quite different. So the way

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:33.030
that I use cloud code and the files and

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:34.870
the folders that I have in that robo

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:37.190
workspace is probably different versus

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:39.110
yours. So I think what's most helpful is

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:40.790
if I give you the principles of how you

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:42.870
can guide Fable to create the perfect

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:44.630
second brain for your system. But in

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:46.389
case you're curious, the design system

00:09:46.399 --> 00:09:48.470
that it created for me is also pretty

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:49.590
interesting. So I'll just go through

00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:51.110
that for a bit because basically what it

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:52.949
did is every time I interact with it or

00:09:52.959 --> 00:09:55.030
I ask it to retrieve a piece from our

00:09:55.040 --> 00:09:57.030
memory, it uses this custom code that it

00:09:57.040 --> 00:09:58.870
created called brain.js stands for

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:00.389
JavaScript. And if I pull back that

00:10:00.399 --> 00:10:01.829
session from earlier, you can see here

00:10:01.839 --> 00:10:04.790
that it is invoking that brain.js at the

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:06.630
beginning of this retrieval task. And

00:10:06.640 --> 00:10:08.550
what it does after is quite simple.

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:11.110
Let's say we ask it which TTS voice we

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:12.949
used. It essentially just strips down

00:10:12.959 --> 00:10:15.030
the keywords there which is just TTS

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:16.470
voice in this case and other filler

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:19.190
words like which do we use those are

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:20.790
discarded and at this point for all of

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:22.949
these the reason why it is able to save

00:10:22.959 --> 00:10:24.470
on tokens is because this is

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:26.310
deterministic code so it's not really

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:28.870
invoking the model in order to do all of

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:30.470
these things because I have a lot of

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:32.870
indexes and reference maps in my own

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:34.870
workspace in my own second brain it can

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:36.710
actually do this where it gives the

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:39.110
possible sources a score without opening

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:41.110
or necessarily reading any of them yet.

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:42.710
And then when it finds the highest

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:44.550
score, basically the file or the

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:46.310
markdown file that has the highest

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:48.230
likelihood where that particular

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:50.470
information is stored, then it opens

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:52.310
that. But it doesn't read the whole

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:53.910
thing. It actually checks if there's a

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:55.910
particular section where that answer may

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:58.150
be obtained. So it works in multiple

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:00.949
layers in order to just prevent it from

00:11:00.959 --> 00:11:02.870
reading irrelevant files. And then it

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:04.870
says here if the section is a pointer to

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:06.230
somewhere else, then the code just

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:07.670
follows that pointer. Again, this is

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:09.509
deterministic code. And then once it has

00:11:09.519 --> 00:11:11.910
that answer, it now provides that to the

00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:14.470
LLM, which is now the model answers back

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:16.150
to you. But like I said, that's the

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:18.310
system that works for me. It may not

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:20.470
work for you necessarily. And so the

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:22.389
principle of how you can guide Fable 5

00:11:22.399 --> 00:11:24.550
in order to do this for you is probably

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:26.150
more useful. And the core of it really

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:27.910
is two things. The first one is I just

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:30.870
prompted CloudFable 5 to do this/last 30

00:11:30.880 --> 00:11:32.949
days skill, which is this skill by Matt

00:11:32.959 --> 00:11:34.949
Van Horn, who is the co-founder of Lyft.

00:11:34.959 --> 00:11:36.870
And what that does essentially is it

00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:39.110
will scour the web across Reddit, across

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:41.430
Twitter, YouTube, Hacker News and other

00:11:41.440 --> 00:11:43.269
several sources in order to find at

00:11:43.279 --> 00:11:45.269
least for this case the best practices

00:11:45.279 --> 00:11:47.110
when it comes to second brain system. So

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:48.630
I just had it pull and do a deep

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:50.310
research on that information and use

00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:52.310
that information as well as scanning our

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:53.990
workspace in order to create that system

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:55.750
for us. The other thing is because I'm a

00:11:55.760 --> 00:11:57.269
bit closer to this space, I actually

00:11:57.279 --> 00:11:59.509
know of a few memory projects which are

00:11:59.519 --> 00:12:01.670
these open- source repositories that I

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:03.670
think would be good resources for Fable.

00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:05.030
So if you want to use these as well, you

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:06.790
can just take a screenshot of this and

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:08.710
include that in your prompt to fable 5.

00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:10.790
To give an example, this QMD project,

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:12.550
which I think stands for query my docs,

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:13.910
that's from Toby, the founder of

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:15.670
Shopify, which essentially just applies

00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:17.910
the principle of semantic search to your

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:19.750
memory system. There is this system by

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:22.230
Gary Tan, the CEO of Y Combinator on

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:24.069
what he uses as his second brain, which

00:12:24.079 --> 00:12:25.750
he calls Gbrain. And then there is

00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:27.910
another open source repo that is now

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:29.590
actually funded by Y combinator called

00:12:29.600 --> 00:12:31.750
graphify that just creates smarter

00:12:31.760 --> 00:12:33.430
connections between your files and

00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:35.990
folders. So I just gave all these three

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:38.230
to fable and to ask it to go through

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:40.310
these repositories, consider them and

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:41.910
build out the perfect second brain

00:12:41.920 --> 00:12:43.829
system for my workspace. And the other

00:12:43.839 --> 00:12:45.350
helpful thing when it comes to creating

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:47.030
at least this visual is that you can

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:49.670
actually ask Fable to set a slashgo to

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:50.710
make sure that let's say if you're

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:52.230
changing layouts in here or you're

00:12:52.240 --> 00:12:54.150
moving nodes around, it can actually

00:12:54.160 --> 00:12:55.509
check its own work to make sure that

00:12:55.519 --> 00:12:57.750
there is no lag for the user. Similar

00:12:57.760 --> 00:12:59.670
with every time that I reload this and

00:12:59.680 --> 00:13:01.990
it refreshes. I also set a slash goal

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:03.350
there in order to make sure that it

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:05.110
loads under 10 seconds and that it

00:13:05.120 --> 00:13:07.350
should continuously optimize the whole

00:13:07.360 --> 00:13:09.190
interface up until it gets to that

00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:11.190
point. So that's just one way for you to

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:13.190
improve I guess the user experience if

00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:14.790
this visual view is something that you

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:16.870
are after. But there you go. This is my

00:13:16.880 --> 00:13:19.670
second brain system. It is I think a

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:22.150
good way for you to use Claude Fable 5

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:24.629
while it's here in the non-usagebased

00:13:24.639 --> 00:13:26.310
pricing. It's a really powerful model

00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:27.910
and if you're looking to upgrade your

00:13:27.920 --> 00:13:29.670
second brain system and the way that

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:31.509
your context and your memory is set up,

00:13:31.519 --> 00:13:33.030
then I think you can offload a lot of

00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:35.190
that work while Fable is part of your

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:37.519
subscription.
